  "question": {
    "text": "A particle of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> moves in circular orbits with potential energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>U</mi><mfenced><mi>r</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>r</mi></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> is a positive constant and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> is its distance from the origin. Its energies are calculated using the Bohr model. If the radius of the particle&#39;s orbit is denoted by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and its speed and energy are denoted by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math>&nbsp;respectively, then for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>th</mi></msup></math> orbit (here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math> is the Planck&#39;s constant)"
